    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "title",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "# chk3 Checkpoint Selection"
      },
      {
        "id": "technical_summary",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "analysis_snapshot_source",
        "body": "## Checkpoint 250 is the strongest automatic-gate choice\n\n**Select checkpoint 250.** It owns the run minimum eval loss (`1.121203661`), passes all `3/3` frozen generation cases, and has the lowest mean (`0.1393`) and maximum (`0.1736`) repetition among loss-eligible checkpoints that pass every hard gate. Checkpoint 230 is rejected because its long case omits four source dates; checkpoint 318 is valid but adds no validation benefit and has slightly higher repetition."
      },
      {
        "id": "headline",
        "type": "metric-strip",
        "cardIds": [
          "selected_cp_card",
          "best_eval_card",
          "valid_cases_card",
          "repetition_card"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "loss_finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "loss_history_source",
        "body": "## Validation improvement stops after the second epoch\n\nEval loss falls steadily through step 230, then stays within a `0.000147` band through step 318. The third epoch continues lowering average train loss while validation is effectively flat, so later exposure is not justified unless generation quality improves."
      },
      {
        "id": "eval_chart_block",
        "type": "chart",
        "chartId": "eval_loss_chart",
        "layout": "full"
      },
      {
        "id": "generation_finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "analysis_snapshot_source",
        "body": "## Generation gates separate near-identical loss points\n\nLoss alone would treat cp230, cp250, and cp318 as nearly interchangeable. The frozen probe does not: cp230 fails exact date preservation on the long sample. cp250 passes EOS, cap, boundary, paragraph, numeric, date, and periodic-tail checks on all three samples, while remaining only `+0.0136` above the chk1 baseline in mean repetition."
      },
      {
        "id": "rep_chart_block",
        "type": "chart",
        "chartId": "repetition_chart",
        "layout": "full"
      },
      {
        "id": "candidate_table_block",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "candidate_table",
        "layout": "full"
      },
      {
        "id": "definitions",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## What the gate measures\n\nA checkpoint first has to finish with EOS before 3,072 tokens, emit exactly one `</think>`, produce a non-empty one-paragraph Minutes answer, preserve the source numeric multiset and date set exactly, and avoid a strict periodic tail. It must also remain below the pre-registered repetition ceilings and sit within `0.001` eval loss of the run minimum. Loss is a shortlist metric; output integrity is the selection gate."
      },
      {
        "id": "methodology",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "analysis_snapshot_source",
        "body": "## Identical inputs make checkpoint differences interpretable\n\nThe comparison uses the same three validation rows (short, medium, long), chk1 base model, tokenizer, greedy decoding, 3,072-token cap, seed, and probe implementation for every checkpoint. The executed `fomc_trainer` notebook independently reconstructs all 318 train records, 32 eval records, five checkpoint summaries, per-case deltas, and the selected checkpoint."
      },
      {
        "id": "limitations",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## The choice is regression-safe, not a full semantic certification\n\nThe probe contains only three deterministic cases and deliberately stores hashes and structural metrics rather than completion text. It therefore does not manually judge non-numeric factual claims, causal reasoning, omissions, or prose style. The underlying direct chk1→chk3 run is also explicitly standalone and non-promotable under the canonical DAG."
      },
      {
        "id": "next_steps",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Use cp250 for the next evaluation artifact\n\n1. Bind downstream evaluation or merge work to the exact checkpoint-250 adapter hash.\n2. Run a larger held-out, text-reviewable Minutes evaluation before any promotion claim.\n3. Keep cp200 as the conservative fallback and cp170 as the earlier regression control.\n4. Do not substitute the root adapter: it is byte-equivalent to cp318, not cp250."
      },
      {
        "id": "further_questions",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Further questions\n\nWould checkpoint 250 retain its advantage on all 190 held-out atomic rows, and does its final Minutes prose avoid unsupported non-numeric claims? Those checks could change confidence in promotion, but they do not change the current five-checkpoint regression-gate winner."
      }
    ]
  },
